    "body": "Stacked on #1591 (`configuration`).\n\nThe Colors slide's type used to *be* the CSS variable list with Hazel syntax on\nit: `expected_type` was generated from lists of raw custom-property names, and\nthe header said so outright \u2014 \"The label IS the CSS custom-property name,\nverbatim.\" So a themer edited CSS internals: `BR2`, `T3`, `shard-caret-tpat`,\n`GB1`, and a misspelling (`backback-targets`) that was load-bearing because it\nwas a CSS name.\n\nThis gives the theme an explicit, semantic representation and maps it onto CSS,\nthen collapses the three stacked CSS vocabularies into one.\n\n## What changed\n\n**The shape of a theme is a type.** `BuiltinsColorScheme.re` holds it as Hazel\ntypes in the builtin context \u2014 `ColorSeeds`, `ColorPalette`, `ColorRoles`,\n`ColorScheme` \u2014 and the slide writes them in its own signatures:\n`let theme_of(p: ColorPalette, role: ColorOverrides): ColorScheme`.\n`ColorRamp`/`ColorOverrides` are declared in the slide, being seams between its\nown two functions. That puts each check on the record a mistake is in; without\nit the only analysis is the whole-program one, which lands on the final `case`\nas a single inconsistency between two enormous products.\n\n**The slide names roles**, grouped by the part of the UI they dress (`menu`,\n`editor`, `cursor`, `hole`, `probe`, \u2026), with a fan-out table in\n`ColorConfiguration.re` expanding each field to the CSS properties that carry\nit \u2014 one field per *decision*, several targets where several properties carry\none decision. ~~`expected_type` and\n`all_targets` both derive from that table, so they cannot drift apart.~~\n`all_targets` derives from that table; `expected_type` is the named\n`ColorScheme`, and a test pins that the slide fills it exactly.\n\nThe two layers differ by **reuse, not derivation**: a palette color is one the\ntheme is built out of, fanned out to several properties at once; a role is one\njob a color is put to, usually carrying one. Roles that only forwarded a\nstated color are palette entries now, renamed for what they are (`exp-base` \u2192\n`probe-value`, `main-base` \u2192 `statics-background`).\n\n**~~The palette took the slide's names: `--Y1` \u2192 `--attention-2`, `--T1` \u2192\n`--surface-1`, `--SAND` \u2192 `--code-background`.~~** The rename landed, and then\nthe names went: nothing read `--ink`, `--frame-1` or the rest \u2014 no stylesheet,\nno OCaml, no script, and the only references left in the tree were commented\nout. A palette color now reaches CSS only under the semantic names the fan-out\ngives it, which makes \"components consume roles, not the palette\" structural\nrather than lint-enforced.\n\n**`roles.css` is deleted.** It was a third vocabulary: hand-written aliases\ncarrying nearly half the component `var()` sites, and its names resolved to far\nfewer palette colors than there were names, so `--text-faint`,\n`--border-soft` and `--shadow-soft` were one color with no way to separate\nthem. Every entry was a bare alias, so they fold into the fan-out table and the\ntheme writes them itself. ~~**No component stylesheet changed** \u2014 every site\nreads the same names as before.~~ None changed for *that*; elsewhere on the\nbranch some sites were renamed off the raw palette names.\n\n**`variables.css` has no hand-written color left**: type, timing and the\nz-index ladder. ~~Type, a generated block (`make update-css-defaults`, guarded\nby a test), and the z-index ladder.~~ The generated defaults are their own\nlabelled file, `style/theme-generated.css`, byte-identical to what the theme\nsets so nothing shifts when it lands.\n\n**The gap is closed.** The colors the theme did not own were dead, pure\nindirection, or derived by CSS itself (the `doc` hue rotations, the\n`incremental-pending` fades); they are decided in the slide now.\n\n**Two flags, and they compose.** `--hazel-color-scheme` feeds the standard\n`color-scheme` property; `--hazel-contrast` is new, so a stylesheet can answer\nhigh contrast with a style query. Four schemes from two booleans, one `case`.\n\n**Also fixed:** `color-scheme` was set nowhere, so native scrollbars rendered\nlight on a dark editor; the two flags overrode each other instead of composing;\n`proj-fold.css` had an inert `color: --var(BLACK)`; and table rows and headers\nwere near-illegible in high contrast, because a row highlight was placed on the\npage's contrast axis when table rows sit on the code background.\n\n**The slide reads better.** It opens with every scheme but the first folded;\ntuple extension instead of restating records, direct function definitions, and\na smaller `ColorOverrides` \u2014 several of its fields turned out to be one\ndecision spelled a few ways. `style/README.md` is new, and documents the\ndataflow, file ownership and both layers.\n\n## How it is checked\n\n- **Golden** (`test/goldens/colors.tsv`): every property \u00d7 every scheme.\n  Written sorted by name, so a pure restructuring leaves it and\n  `theme-generated.css` byte-identical \u2014 which is how every refactor after the\n  vocabulary work was verified.\n- **Reverse substitution** for renames: mapping the new names back reproduces\n  every touched file byte for byte.\n- **Chrome**, at each stage: every variable resolves to the golden in all four\n  schemes, and a stylesheet-only page with no JavaScript resolves them all to\n  the light column.\n- **`./hazel analyze hazel-programs/config/colors.hz`** names the offending\n  term when an annotation stops holding.\n- `lint_css_roles.py` rewritten for the two-layer shape: components read roles\n  not the palette, only the generated file declares a theme-owned color on\n  `:root`, and the dangling-reference ratchet stays.\n\n## Known gaps\n\n- Folding kept \"one field per decision\", so `--text-faint` and `--border-soft`\n  still come off one `palette.frame-1` row. Splitting a case is a two-file\n  change; a field per property was rejected as worse to read.\n- Two further override merges would each move a color by one ramp step, so\n  they want an eye rather than a green test: collapsing the `frame-*` dials,\n  and merging `projector.textarea-text` into `inspector.text`.\n- **TODO:** we still need some way of enforcing that newly added colors stay\n  themeable. The lint catches a component declaring a theme-owned color on\n  `:root`, but not a new hardcoded literal in a rule.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n",
